---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is melanocortin.com medical advice?',
    a: 'No. It is an educational reference. Nothing here is a diagnosis, a treatment recommendation or a protocol, and the site deliberately publishes no dosing, sourcing or usage instructions for any drug, approved or otherwise. Take what you learn here to a qualified clinician.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who is behind the site?',
    a: 'An independent editorial project, developed in the open on GitHub under the utilityincoming account. Pages are written from primary literature and regulatory documents, not paraphrased from other websites. The site does not claim named medical reviewers it does not have; see “Who writes and edits this” for exactly what is and is not claimed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know a claim is accurate?',
    a: 'Follow the numbered citation. Every substantive claim links to a reference at the foot of the page — a PubMed-indexed paper, a regulatory record or, for trial status, a dated company disclosure labelled as such. Reference identifiers are checked against PubMed before a page ships. If a citation does not support the sentence it is attached to, that is a correctable error; please report it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why are dates shown only to the year?',
    a: 'Because a year is a claim the site can stand behind. “Last updated 2026” means the page was checked against current sources this year. A day-precise “reviewed on” stamp implies a formal review event that, for most pages, has not yet happened, and the site will not imply it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the site take money from drug companies or peptide vendors?',
    a: 'No. There is no sponsorship, no paid placement, no affiliate link and no vendor relationship. If any commercial relationship is ever introduced it will be disclosed on the pages where it appears.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I reuse the text and figures?',
    a: 'Yes. Text and figures are published under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 and the code under the MIT licence. Credit melanocortin.com and link back; for figures, the SVG source is in the public repository.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I report an error?',
    a: 'Open an issue on the GitHub repository with the page, the sentence and, ideally, the source that contradicts it. Corrections are tracked in the open and credited in the commit history.',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Editorial standards"
  kicker="About"
  description="Accuracy, honesty, and traceable sources are the whole point of the project. Here is exactly what the site covers, how pages are written, sourced, illustrated, reviewed and corrected, what it deliberately will not do, and how to reuse or cite it."
  updated="2026"
>
  <p class="page-lede">
    melanocortin.com is an open, educational reference on the melanocortin
    system — the POMC-derived peptides, the MC1R–MC5R receptors, and the
    therapeutics that target them. It is written to serve three audiences on the
    same pages: students and researchers who need mechanism and citations;
    clinicians and curious readers who want an accurate plain-language picture;
    and anyone arriving from a search engine who deserves a credible answer
    rather than a content farm.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because much of this material is health-related, the standard we hold
    ourselves to is higher than "sounds right." Every substantive claim should be
    traceable to a primary source, uncertainty should be stated rather than
    hidden, and the writing should never sell you anything. This page explains how
    we try to live up to that — and where we have not yet got there.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
  <p>
    The melanocortin system is one of the best-understood signalling families in
    human biology and one of the worst-served on the open web. The science lives
    in dense journals on one side; on the other, a grey market in injectable
    peptides has produced a large volume of hype, half-truths and forum lore, much
    of it written to sell. Between the two there is very little: a reader who
    wants to know what MC4R actually does, why a tanning peptide causes nausea, or
    what a Phase 3 result did and did not show has nowhere credible to go that is
    also readable. The GLP-1 era has pulled a large audience into caring about the
    biology of appetite as participants rather than spectators, and for that
    audience the melanocortin receptors are the next node worth understanding.
    This site is the in-between: rigorous enough to cite, plain enough to read.
  </p>

  <h2>What the site covers, and what it does not</h2>
  <p>
    The scope is the system and the medicines built on it. That means the{' '}
    <a href="/system">POMC peptides</a> and how they are cut; the{' '}
    <a href="/receptors">five receptors</a>, each on its own page; the
    endogenous brakes <a href="/asip">ASIP</a> and <a href="/agrp">AgRP</a>; the
    receptors' <a href="/structures">structural biology</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/binding-matrix">pharmacology</a>; the physiology they run —
    pigment, appetite, the adrenal axis, inflammation, sodium balance; the{' '}
    <a href="/genetics">genetics</a> of what happens when they vary; every
    approved drug in <a href="/therapeutics">therapeutics</a> and every
    investigational one in the <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a>; and the
    grey-market compounds, <a href="/melanotan">melanotan II</a> and PT-141,
    treated as pharmacology to be understood rather than products to be sourced.
  </p>
  <p>
    What it does not cover is deliberate. There are no dosing charts, no cycle
    guides, no vendor comparisons, no reconstitution instructions and no
    "protocols," for approved drugs or unapproved ones. Those pages exist
    elsewhere and are precisely the content this site was built as an alternative
    to. Where a page discusses a grey-market compound, it explains what the
    molecule does and why its effects are inseparable from its mechanism, and it
    stops there.
  </p>

  <h2>Who writes and edits this</h2>
  <p>
    Pages are researched, written, and maintained by the melanocortin.com
    editorial team. We write from the primary literature and regulatory
    documents — not by paraphrasing other secondary sites — and we do not use
    invented credentials or fictional "medical reviewers" to borrow authority we
    haven't earned.
  </p>
  <p>
    We are formalising named authorship and independent expert review. Until a
    qualified reviewer has actually signed off on a page, that page is dated
    <strong>"Last updated,"</strong> not "Last reviewed" — the two are different
    claims, and we will not blur them. When named, credentialed review is in
    place, it will appear on the page and be described here.
  </p>
  <p>
    Drafting is assisted by AI writing tools, and the repository's commit history
    records that openly. What the tools do not do is decide what is true. Every
    reference is resolved against PubMed by its identifier and checked against the
    title, journal and year it is cited as; every claim about a trial is checked
    against the trial's own publication or disclosure; and a human editor reviews
    and merges every change before it is published. A citation that does not
    support its sentence is a defect we want to hear about, whatever produced it.
  </p>

  <h2>What a page must have before it ships</h2>
  <p>
    Each content page is built to the same shape, so that a reader who has learned
    to use one can use any of them.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A byline and a dated last-update</strong>, shown at the top. The
      date is the year the page was last checked against current sources.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Key takeaways</strong> — the four to six sentences a reader in a
      hurry needs, written to be correct on their own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Numbered citations in the text</strong>, each resolving to a
      reference at the foot of the page with a link to the original.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Plain statements of uncertainty.</strong> Where the science is
      early, contested or extrapolated from animals, the page says so in the
      sentence, not in a footnote.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A "what this is not" note</strong> on every drug and peptide page,
      restating that the page is education, not a protocol.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Common questions</strong>, answered in a paragraph each, for the
      reader who arrived with one specific question.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we source claims</h2>
  <p>
    Claims of fact link to a citeable source so you can check the original rather
    than take our word for it. In rough order of preference, we rely on:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Peer-reviewed primary literature and authoritative reviews</strong>
      — accessed through PubMed / PMC, and cited with PMIDs or DOIs. Primary
      papers are preferred to reviews, and the original discovery is cited
      alongside the modern synthesis where both matter.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Regulatory documents</strong> — FDA approvals and prescribing
      information, and equivalent agency records, for anything about what a drug
      is approved to do.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Primary company disclosures</strong> — trial results, press
      releases and filings, used for pipeline status when nothing peer-reviewed
      yet exists, and always labelled as the interested-party sources they are.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reputable trade and medical press</strong> — for regulatory events
      such as an application being accepted, where the news report is the most
      accessible dated record.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We prefer the strongest available evidence, name the source in the text where
    it matters, and treat press releases and marketing as claims to verify, not
    facts to repeat. Two kinds of source are not used: aggregated topic pages
    that summarise other summaries, and anything that is itself trying to sell
    the compound it describes.
  </p>

  <h2>How figures are made</h2>
  <p>
    Every diagram on the site is original: drawn as code, in the open, for the
    page it sits on. None are stock images or redrawn from a paper's figure.
    Each is a schematic of a mechanism — how MRAP escorts MC2R to the surface, why
    the mouse cannot make β-MSH, where the γ-MSH salt loop breaks — with a
    caption that states what it shows and a source line naming the papers the
    schematic is drawn after. Figures are checked in both light and dark mode so
    that no label is lost in either, and they carry text descriptions for readers
    using screen readers. Where a figure simplifies, the caption says so;
    "schematic, not to scale" means exactly that.
  </p>

  <h2>How pages are reviewed and kept current</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every page carries a visible byline and a dated last-update.</li>
    <li>
      Therapeutic and clinical-pipeline information dates quickly, so it is
      verified against current primary sources before publication and revisited
      as the field moves. The <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a> page is a dated
      snapshot and says so; it lists discontinued programmes as well as live
      ones, because in this field the failures explain the successes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Dates are shown to the year. We would rather commit to an honest
      "updated this year" than imply a false-precision timestamp we can't stand
      behind.
    </li>
    <li>
      Where the science is uncertain, contested, or genuinely unknown, the page
      says so — plainly — rather than papering over it. Animal findings are
      labelled as animal findings; a single small trial is called a signal, not a
      result.
    </li>
    <li>
      The full history of every page is public in the repository's commit log,
      so a reader can see what changed, when, and on what evidence.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Independence and funding</h2>
  <p>
    melanocortin.com is editorially independent. It is not sponsored by, and does
    not take payment from, any pharmaceutical company, compounder, or vendor to
    cover, favour, downplay, or omit a drug or product. What appears here is
    driven by what is scientifically and practically true, not by who might pay
    for coverage.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the site ever introduces affiliate links or other commercial
    relationships, they will be <strong>clearly disclosed on the pages where they
    appear</strong>, they will not influence what we say about a product, and
    they will never turn an educational page into a sales pitch. Our bias is
    stated up front and it is a simple one: toward the reader.
  </p>

  <h2>Not medical advice</h2>
  <p>
    Content is for education and general information only. It is not medical
    advice, diagnosis, or treatment, and it is no substitute for a qualified
    clinician. We do not provide dosing, sourcing, or usage instructions for any
    drug, approved or otherwise. Never disregard professional medical advice, or
    delay seeking it, because of something you read here. If you are considering
    any of the compounds discussed here, including the unapproved ones, the
    right next step is a conversation with a clinician who knows your history,
    not a forum.
  </p>

  <h2 id="corrections" class="scroll-mt-24">Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Found an error or an out-of-date claim? Corrections are welcome and credited.
    The fastest way to reach us is to{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/utilityincoming/melanocortin/issues">open an issue
    on GitHub</a>, where changes are tracked in the open. The most useful report
    names the page, quotes the sentence, and points to the source that contradicts
    it. We treat a well-sourced correction as a gift, not a nuisance — flagging a
    mistake makes the reference better for everyone who reads it next.
  </p>

  <h2>Licensing and how to cite</h2>
  <p>
    Site content — text and diagrams — is published under{' '}
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons
    Attribution 4.0 (CC BY 4.0)</a>. The source code is released under the MIT
    license. Reuse it; just credit the source. A sufficient citation names the
    page, the site and the year shown on the page — for example, "MC2R — the
    ACTH receptor. melanocortin.com, 2026." — with a link. The figures' SVG source
    is in the public repository and may be adapted under the same licence.
  </p>

  <FAQ items={faqs} />
</PageLayout>
